# campus-fix v2.7: TCP fingerprint + behavioral counters (fw4 nftables drop-in)
#
# Targets the remaining "proxy/router detected" vectors the v2.x rules don't
# cover. The trigger banner was: "发现您当前网络环境存在代理行为" - these
# ruleset families are the classic causes on a NAT router with many LAN
# devices behind one session:
#
#   campus_mss_clamp   - per-OS TCP MSS fingerprint mixing
#   campus_synrate     - new-connection burst signature (flow-rate stats)
#   campus_connlimit   - concurrent flow count per LAN host (flow-count stats)
#
# All three are default-on: they are invisible in single-host use and only
# bound the aggregate to what one busy PC produces.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- MSS clamp
# Mechanism: every OS negotiates a different TCP MSS (Windows 1460, iOS/macOS
# 1440, many Androids 1400-1440). Behind one NAT IP a detector sees MANY
# distinct MSS values on parallel flows - a direct giveaway that one IP is
# fronting several different devices. clamping MSS to ONE value on all
# WAN-bound SYNs makes the whole LAN negotiate like a single OS.
# 1460 = the plain-Ethernet Windows value (matches our TTL=128 baseline).
# If you switch the TTL baseline to 64 (Linux/Mac profile), change this to
# 1440 as well.
#
# mangle FORWARD is used (not postrouting): the clamp must rewrite the
# FORWARDED SYN itself. The MTU route method is used because it is
# PMTU-safe: established flows with a smaller real path-MTU still get
# correct clamping via the route metric.
chain campus_mss_clamp {
	type filter hook forward priority mangle; policy accept;
	oifname != "lo" tcp flags syn tcp option maxseg size set rt mtu counter comment "campus-fix: clamp MSS to path MTU (uniform 1460)"
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------ SYN rate limit
# Mechanism: one host opens connections at human/web-page rates (a few SYN/s
# even when loading a heavy site). A NAT with N devices bursts far higher,
# and flow-rate anomaly detectors flag exactly that. Meter NEW connections
# per LAN source and drop only the excess above the burst. 40 syn/s with a
# 100-packet burst is above any single browser session but far below what a
# small LAN spikes to - tune in README.
#
# rate must be a plain literal in nft; the values below were chosen for the
# 128MB RM2100 (few dozen devices max).
chain campus_synrate {
	type filter hook forward priority filter - 4; policy accept;
	iifname "br-lan" oifname != "lo" tcp flags syn ct state new meter campus_synrate4 { ip saddr limit rate over 40/second burst 100 packets } counter drop comment "campus-fix: per-host SYN rate limit"
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------ conn limit
# Mechanism: flow-count statistics. A single PC holds O(300) concurrent
# connections at worst (P2P aside); a NAT'd LAN looks like an office. Cap
# the sustained NEW-connection rate per LAN host - a proxy for both the
# concurrent-flow count and the "office building" flow-rate profile. 600
# new connections per minute per host (10/s sustained) is above any normal
# browsing session (even a heavy page load is a burst, not sustained) but
# far below what a NAT'd LAN or a P2P client emits.
#
# A meter is used (same as campus_synrate): meters are anonymous dynamic
# sets fully parsed by nft userspace, so fw4 loads the rule even where
# ct-count-in-set (conntrack state in set update) is unavailable.
chain campus_connlimit {
	type filter hook forward priority filter - 3; policy accept;
	iifname "br-lan" oifname != "lo" tcp flags syn ct state new meter campus_connrate4 { ip saddr limit rate over 600/minute burst 200 packets } counter drop comment "campus-fix: per-host new-flow rate cap"
}
